some potions that I don't want to polymorph, and typically I find it
more rewarding to dilute them for holy water. After all the outcome is
also just gambling, though with a good probability in case of healing.

Polymorphing potions is in my games only a side effect of the late game
where I try to poly for some, say, missing ring and put the superfluous
potions (and scrolls and wands) on top of the stack. I do that at a stage
where it doesn't hurt if the potions disappear in the polymorph process.

> And even if that doesn't result in enough full healing potions, can't
> you use the castle wand to reverse genocide nurses?

Once at the Castle you can indeed compensate missing items using wishes.
The problem is also *not* that I haven't enough stuff to do alchemy in
all my games, rather I mostly do alchemy, at least after having crossed
Gehennom the first time. The point is more that (IME) an assumption that
"at point X in the game you have item Y in quantities Z" is not one you
can rely on, even if Z is as small as 1; there have been too many games
where I was lacking some specific item (even items with a high random
generation probability) or a specific monster (e.g. a floating eye for
ESP, a gray dragon for scales, etc.). And there are games where I get
an abundance of *rare* items; tons of magic lamps, bags of holding, etc.

